{"name":"Natural Twine","hex":"#dbc39b","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"natural","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Existing in nature.","Normally associated with a particular person or thing; inherently related to the nature of a thing or creature."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native inhabitant of a place, country, etc.","A note that is not or is no longer to be modified by an accidental."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/natural"},{"term":"twine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A twist; a convolution.","A strong thread composed of two or three smaller threads or strands twisted together, and used for various purposes, as for binding small parcels, making nets, and the like; a small cord or string."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To weave together.","To wind, as one thread around another, or as any flexible substance around another body."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/twine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Twine","blocks":[{"text":"Twine is a strong thread, light string or cord composed of string in which two or more thinner strands are twisted, and then twisted together (plied). The strands are plied in the opposite direction to that of their twist, which adds torsional strength to the cord and keeps it from unravelling. This process is sometimes called reverse wrap. The same technique used for making twine is also used to make thread, which is thinner, yarn, and rope, which is stronger and thicker, generally with three or more strands."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Multiple_thread.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twine"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Multiple_thread.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Multiple_thread.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Twine showing component strands","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMultiple_thread.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg/500px-Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg/1280px-Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg/500px-Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Men making twine. Japan, 1915","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaking_Twine_(1915_by_Elstner_Hilton).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Baling_twine%2C_hay_bale.jpg/500px-Baling_twine%2C_hay_bale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Baling_twine%2C_hay_bale.jpg/500px-Baling_twine%2C_hay_bale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Binder twine securing a hay bale","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABaling_twine%2C_hay_bale.jpg"}],"sounds":{"natural":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-natural.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/3/35/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Persephone_Amethyst_%28NaomiAmethyst%29-natural.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Naomi_Pe